  "strategy": "We are aiming to find a sequence of non-negative floats that minimizes the objective function defined by:\n    score = 2 * n * max(conv(a, a)) / (sum(a))^2\nwhere n is the length of the sequence, and conv(a, a) is the full cross-correlation (mode='full') which produces a vector of length 2*n-1.\n\nHere's a detailed plan to construct a sequence that achieves a lower score than the previous 1.504345:\n\n1. Initial Setup and Global Attributes\n   - Set up a base length, n_base, initially at 1600 (the parent length if provided, otherwise a starting point)\n   - Maintain a candidate multiplier to control the sequence length dynamically: around 0.8 to 1.2 of current_base_length\n\n2. Starting Candidates\n   - Bootstrap from a parent sequence if available, normalized as necessary\n   - If no parent, start with four fundamental sequences of length 1600 with different distributions to ensure a wide exploration:\n        - Style 'a': cos^2 based (highly oscillatory near boundaries)\n        - Style 'b': exponential dipole (peaks at both ends, dips in center)\n        - Style 'c': smooth sinusoidal with moderate rounding\n        - Style 'd': alternating exponential and sinusoidal peaks\n\n3. Core Optimization Loop\n   - Hybrid Approach: Use both LP relaxation and coordinate descent toggled between 30% and 50% ratios based on current improvement\n   - Wald Identity: Regularly re-normalize the sequence geometry by decomposing extrema points\n   - Nesterov Acceleration: Blend momentum updates to avoid local minima\n\n4. Advanced Traits\n   - Sparse Operator Techniques: Apply coordinate pruning when sequence length exceeds 3000\n   - Dynamic Mode Exploitation: Activate full cross-correlation in mode 'sparse' for efficiency when sequence has high magnitude\n   - Cross-Entropy Constraints: Terminate if object gradient exceeds 1.5e-3 per iteration.\n\n5. InterpolationPhase\n   - Contraction and Expansion: Switch between base lengths with factors of 2 (increase or decrease candidate multiplier)\n   - Golden-Section Search: Verify new positions using rigorous geometric optimization, even for length-shifted sequences\n   - Direction-Based Selection: Maintain remembered sequences from successful changes\n\n6. Final Convergence Check\n   - With 300 seconds remaining, run a final optimized cycle focusing on numerical stability\n   - Full Cross-Validation using convolution to ensure best candidate doesn't violate bounds\n   - Time-based termination to secure the solution\n\nImplementation Details:\n\nWe'll implement the following steps in the code:\n\n- Initialize by setting the base length and creating four candidate sequences for optimization\n- Use Nesterov-accelerated LP as the primary optimization method with periodic coordinate descent boosts\n- Dynamic restarts are handled by varying the base length and rerunning optimization on new candidates\n\nHelper Functions:\n\n- `get_best_solver`: To handle LP selection based on sequence length (use existing for safety)\n- `fast_autocorr`: The core evaluator function for sequence score (mode='full')\n- `structured_init`: Generate four fundamental patterns\n- `interpolate_seq`: Create candidates in different lengths (reserved as fallback)\n- `perturb_seq`: Construct initial seeds\n\nMain Algorithm:\n\n1. Load parent sequence if available (skip normalization if sum > sqrt(2*base_length))\n2. If no parent, initialize using one of four styles with base_length=1600\n3. Set most recent sequence as initial candidate\n4. Optimize using a hybrid method (LP and CD) allocated 75% of time\n5. When score improvement stalls, enter a multi-length probing phase (remaining 25% of time)\n   - Double base length (geometric resampling, must re-norm after expanding)\n   - Halve base length (re-norm after contraction)\n   - Use linear interpolation to maintain continuity across dimensions\n6. Validate all found sequences against the evaluator to ensure they don't clip to invalid numbers\n\nThe iteration uses a Nesterov momentum factor of (1/2 + iterations^-0.5) for exponential decay.\n\nConstraint Handling:\n\n- All sequences normalized to have sum = sqrt(2 * base_length) before evaluation to ensure the multiplier is the dominant score factor\n- Clipping to 0 and 1000 during scoring (handled by evaluate_sequence) - but during search, we avoid poor candidates\n\nTerminology:\n\n- \"Best score\" is 2*n*max(conv) / sum^2\n- \"Stall\" indicates no score improvement for 40 consecutive ones\n- \"Initializing\" uses four distinct styles to prevent early convergence\n\nfallback:\n\nIn case all optimizations fail, return a uniform sequence normalized to the base length.\n\nLet's implement accordingly.",
